d>B</bold>) Relapse-free survival rates were significantly different for patients with low (&#x02212; or &#x0002b;) and high (&#x0002b;&#x0002b; or &#x0002b;&#x0002b;&#x0002b;) levels of CCR7 immunostaining (<italic>P</italic>&#x0003d;0.0175). (<bold>C</bold>) Patterns of disease relapse according to CCR7 immunostaining.</p></caption><graphic xmlns:xlink="http://www.w3.org/1999/xlink" mime-subtype="eps" xlink:href="6603907f4"/></fig><fig id="fig5"><label>Figure 5</label><caption><p>Overall survival rates were significantly different for patients with low (&#x02212; or &#x0002b;) and high (&#x0002b;&#x0002b; or &#x0002b;&#x0002b;&#x0002b;) levels of CCR7 immunostaining (<italic>P</italic>&#x0003d;0.0136).</p></caption><graphic xmlns:xlink="http://www.w3.org/1999/xlink" mime-subtype="eps" xlink:href="6603907f5"/></fig><fig id="fig6"><label>Figure 6</label><caption><p>Disease-specific survival rates were significantly different for patients with low (&#x02212; or &#x0002b;) and high (&#x0002b;&#x0002b; or &#x0002b;&#x0002b;&#x0002b;) levels of CCR7 immunostaining (<italic>P</italic>&#x0003d;0.0062).</p></caption><graphic xmlns:xlink="http://www.w3.org/1999/xlink" mime-subtype="eps" xlink:href="6603907f6"/></fig><table-wrap id="tbl1" position="float"><label>Table 1</label><caption><p content-type="table-title">Summary of TNM stage at presentation in the 84 patients with squamous cell carcinoma of the tonsil</p></caption><table frame="hsides" rules="groups" border="1" width="85%"><colgroup><col align="left"/><col align="center"/><col align="center"/><col align="center"/><col align="center"/><col align="center"/><col align="center"/><col align="center"/></colgroup><thead valign="bottom"><tr><th align="left" valign="top" charoff="50">&#x000a0;</th><th align="center" valign="top" charoff="50"><bold>N0</bold></th><th align="center" valign="top" charoff="50"><bold>N1</bold></th><th align="center" valign="top" charoff="50"><bold>N2a</bold></th><th align="center" valign="top" c